stems at the scale are certainly  you know there's a lot of uncertainty  and complexity uh  my my answer after all the time i've  been here is just embrace it  right while it's true that these large  distributed systems get kind of hard to  reason about  in our vision of perfection and even if  the basic requirements of the system  are change continuously  but it but it's not just because the  system is big the  uh this uncertainty is also coming from  frankly the evolving nature of our  business  and and our learnings about it and the  fact that we can offer new features over  time the more that we learn  and the more that we build so we can  iterate and  continue as we go this shifts our  thinking towards how to build  technology more organically and  iteratively  which frankly as an engineer is much  more satisfying i've spent over 20 years  again  like being surprised at  uh like what happens next with the  systems that i've built  i always think like oh i solved the  problem we got something out it's  excellent but then  um you know the next year is very  again very surprising and over time i  have begun to look forward to how much  that we can learn and discover  as we go um but from a systems design  perspective this does mean  design for scalability designed for  availability  at the component level at the system  resiliency and recovery level  and and also in the data models and the  apis  like they have to be extensible so that  we can involve them  and and that's just a crucial design  requirement in all of these systems  we're building sometimes  this extensibility is even more  important than  than getting the initial business  requirements correct like  it's okay to start with anything build  momentum and  after we've gotten started then we have  a path to evolve  the system into something that is big  comprehensive  and ultimately impactful to the business  well after 15 years here i'm also  surprised  here over here this thing's the same  over here  um okay so at the scale amazon is  growing  innovation continues to evolve too uh so  i'm curious  aaron how do you approach risk versus  reward  when it comes to innovation improving  the delivery experience for customers  yeah good question um so we have we have  a saying at amazon that it's it's day  one  so even as we grow and diversify we want  to continue to take risks  and continue to invent on behalf of our  customers  but to manage that risk we're often  rolling out these new initiatives  as prototypes that are limited in scope  and if it's working we scale it up to  the entire network  uh quickly and so we've become really  good at doing that scaling  um the good ideas of course come from  everywhere  and anywhere in our org uh  you know we have a very inclusive  planning process where  employees of any level um can contribute  concepts  even if they're for another team's  products or services  and if that idea gets a positive  reception we might write  a prep doc and so for these we try and  imagine  what would the press release say on the  day we launched this  new product and then we think of this  desired state and work backwards from it  and that really enhances and  distinguishes the great ideas it gets  you to think about the risks  and the benefits um you know you have so  many examples  in amazon's history of true innovation  uh even though we're a very large  company now  and i think it's due to some of these  processes so you know from prime  to kindle to aws and alexa  um you know you have current efforts  like kuiper and prime  air and the climate pledge  we don't just have good intentions here  we have mechanisms in place  that promote that risk-taking and  nurture these kinds of big  disruptive innovations and that's  something i love  you know about working here and i think  really sets  amazon apart and  what you said around inventing on behalf  of the customer is really important  we take a customer first view and we  work back from there  for example we know for certain that  customers want faster shipping speeds  and they want specific delivery related  problems to be solved so  once we identify those areas we move  ahead as you described  uh so aaron what's next for robotics ai  uh well we're kind of just at the  beginning for  machine learning and artificial  intelligence  i think there's there's a bunch of  places where we can improve the  experience of our customer  as well as the safety and satisfaction  of our employees  we see right now a lot of one-off use  cases of these technologies where we  write  a lot of specific and tailored code  for a narrow application and i see a  future state where these algorithms  become  you know more readily available and more  accessible  like through aws for example uh  at that point i think it it becomes just  another tool  in our toolbox and you'll see adoption  and innovation and comfort with those  systems  uh tick you know upwards pretty sharply  uh you know as a customer of amazon  personally  i i'm also like super excited about the  electric  uh delivery vans so looking forward to  seeing those  those on the road yeah i  i love aaron's points um it's really  we always think of it as just the  beginning here at amazon  um we're just always constantly trying  to find ways to get orders to customers  faster with lower cost and higher  quality  while keeping our employees safe it's  just what we do every day  um amazon's like john said constantly  changing and growing  and so uh we have to build  with adaptability in mind and develop  technologies that are flexible  and can extend like computer vision  solutions  that can learn to recognize the new  products that show up at amazon every  day  well that wraps up our session and i  want to give a huge thank you to josie  aaron and john  for joining me today and a big thanks to  everyone who tuned in  i hope you all found this helpful and  that you learned a bit more about the  innovation behind  amazon's